app_int/main.py`, and QA adds its tests under `tests/`. I wrote two files in `.dark-factory/runs/307a2621-a090-4b85-99f3-f229dd40b927/artifacts/ARCHITECTURE/`, `design.md` and `constraints.json`, and changed nothing else in the repository. I didn't run a schema validator on `constraints.json`; I wrote it by hand to match the schema (a `constraints` list where each entry has only `id` and `description`).\n\n**What the design says:**\n- **The change:** `subtract(a: int, b: int) -> int` goes directly after `add`, with a one-line docstring, and simply returns `a - b`. It has no input checks. `health_check`, `add` and their tests stay untouched, and no new dependencies are added.\n- **Who writes the test:** the task says \"with a test\", but the Developer can only write under `src/` and QA can only write under `tests/`. So the Developer must not touch `tests/` (an edit there would be reverted), and QA writes the test. The test must include at least one case with a negative result, such as `subtract(3, 5) == -2`, which proves the arguments aren't swapped.\n- **Annotations:** because the module uses `from __future__ import annotations`, `subtract.__annotations__` will hold the string `'int'` rather than the type. AC-7 explicitly accepts that.\n- **Lint:** the test import should read `from app_int.main import add, health_check, subtract`, in that order, so `ruff check` passes.\n- **File path:** the task's `app_int/main.py` is taken to mean the existing `src/app_int/main.py`, as the BA assumed.\n\n`design.md` maps each of the 13 acceptance criteria to the part of the design that satisfies it, and `constraints.json` lists eight constraints (C-1 to C-8), each tied to a requirement or to the write-scope rule. Nothing needs a human decision.","ttft_ms":1338,"type":"result","duration_ms":51305,"uuid":"d4ca4eae-190d-4a88-84ed-f81c7401a2c8","ttft_stream_ms":838,"time_to_request_ms":138,"first_content_frame_ms":838,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
